## What this chart is

`owner-service` wraps the same [`common`](../../library/common/) library that
[`app-template`](../app-template/) consumes, using the same extension point: a
block of computed values merged over the user's values before the library
renders. `app-template`'s block sets one key. This one derives a whole service.

An app declares its **shape** — archetype, port, datastores, dependencies,
workers. The chart derives the Kubernetes objects and the platform environment
from that shape plus a cluster catalog, so an engineer does not hand-write
`DB_HOST`, an assume-role ARN, an egress allow-list or an ExternalSecret mapping.

## Configuration layers

Four tiers stack, in increasing order of authority:

| # | Layer | Owner | Where |
|---|-------|-------|-------|
| 1 | Cluster defaults | Platform | `argocd/defaults/<cluster>.yaml`, loaded as the first `valueFile` |
| 2 | App values | App team, platform-approved | `values.yaml`, `values-<variant>.yaml`, `env.yaml`, `env-<cluster>.yaml` |
| 3 | Chart derivation | Chart | computed from 1 + 2 — **beats** an app's `env:` |
| 4 | `raw:` | App team, platform-reviewed | passthrough to the common library — beats everything, minus the identity exclusions |

Tier 3 is not a file, so `valueFiles` order cannot express this. The merge
happens in `templates/common.yaml`, which owns the precedence.

An app cannot hand-write a key the chart derives: the reserved list in
`templates/_validate.tpl` fails the render instead of letting the derivation win
silently. And `raw:` cannot reach identity — the ServiceAccount name, the
ExternalSecret target, the Gateway/VirtualService/Certificate names, the UST
labels, or `controllers:` — because a rename is delete-then-create rather than a
restart.

**Convention:** every `raw:` block carries a comment saying why it is there. A
JSON schema cannot require YAML comments, so this is a review habit. It is also
the input to the override tally that tells us which patterns to promote into
first-class values.

## Rendering a service locally

There is no live dry-run for this: a second Argo Application rendering into the
same namespace would collide with the running one on every derived name.
`helm template` is the only safe pre-flip validation, which is what makes the
render-diff load-bearing rather than nice-to-have.

```bash
# From a checkout of owner/argocd, with ARGOCD pointing at it.
ARGOCD=~/src/argocd
APP=apps/plg/biz-enrich-service

helm template biz-enrich-service oci://ghcr.io/owner/helm/owner-service \
  --version 0.1.0 \
  --namespace enrichment \
  -f "$ARGOCD/defaults/dev.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/image-pin.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/values.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/values-dev.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/env.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/env-dev.yaml"
```

The `-f` order must match the ApplicationSet's `valueFiles` exactly. Cluster
defaults first, then the image pin, then the app's values, then its environment.

The TLS companion Application is the same command with the `tls-<cluster>`
variant overlay and the ingress-gateway namespace:

```bash
helm template biz-enrich-service oci://ghcr.io/owner/helm/owner-service \
  --version 0.1.0 --namespace istio-ingress \
  -f "$ARGOCD/defaults/dev.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/image-pin.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/values.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/values-tls-dev.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/env.yaml" \
  -f "$ARGOCD/$APP/env-dev.yaml"
```

## Testing

```bash
# Unit tests: every derived value, name, probe and validator.
helm plugin install https://github.com/helm-unittest/helm-unittest.git
helm unittest -f 'unittests/**/*_test.yaml' .

# Install profile: the workload path on bare k3s, no CRDs.
ct install --config ../../../.ci/ct/ct.yaml --charts .
```

Everything CRD-shaped — Gateway, VirtualService, Certificate, ExternalSecret,
ScaledObject — is asserted by `helm unittest`, which suits a chart whose job is
computing values. `ci/minimal-values.yaml` exists because a realistic values
file cannot pass `ct install`: it pulls a private ECR image and names a
ServiceAccount that does not exist in the test cluster, so `helm install --wait`
times out regardless of CRDs.

